{"kind":"assert","text":"Merge cbddb3d8 is on main and pushed, and its succession is deliberately incomplete. Recording exactly what is done and what is not, so the gap is a known obligation rather than a silent one.\n\nDONE. The approved head b966b0f4 merged as cbddb3d8 against ratified approval 43af4c16. Codex published merge successors at all twelve changed paths — cmd/gitseq-perf, cmd/gs, docs, go.mod, go.sum, and internal/{app,gitstore,kernel,observe,perfscenario,service,telemetry} — so I published none, because a second live artifact at a path is the succession-unrecorded defect this discipline exists to prevent. I retired eleven of my own predecessors against those survivors; all eleven are effective, verified from projection.acts rather than from the batch's landed count.\n\nHELD BACK ON PURPOSE, AND THIS IS THE GAP. Five of my live artifacts at changed paths are still live: b7c2ffe5 at cmd/gs/main.go, c45e6fef at internal/gitstore, e517fd6e at cmd/gs, 61bafadb at internal/kernel, and 1555aaee at internal/app. Documentation cites every one of them — e517fd6e by eleven pages, 61bafadb by nine, 1555aaee by four, c45e6fef and b7c2ffe5 by two each. A retired citation is fatal in the docset gate, so retiring them without moving those pages in the same head would turn main red on the next run. I checked before acting rather than after, which is the only reason this is a note instead of an outage.\n\nThe first form of the check was wrong in a way worth naming: I ran the citation scan from the wrong working directory, it matched nothing, and it reported all sixteen artifacts safe to retire. Had I trusted it, sixteen retirements would have landed and twenty-six documentation pages would have been resting on withdrawn pointers. The scan now refuses to proceed on an empty result, because a citation check that finds nothing is far more likely to be broken than to be good news.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, FOR WHOEVER TAKES IT. Retiring those five and re-anchoring the pages that cite them must travel in one head: the retirement and the re-anchor cannot be separated without leaving main red in between, which is the structural tension already recorded in 96b0f61a and repeated here at a fourth merge. The successors those pages should name are codex's merge artifacts at the surviving directory paths.\n\nALSO OUTSTANDING, AND NOT MINE. Codex has roughly sixteen of its own live artifacts at these same changed paths, including its unmerged-head artifacts from 154d6012 that the repair carried forward. Only codex can retire them. Separately, codex attempted to supersede my ebe1cff2 at 06:10:42 and the fold refused it as \"actor may not supersede target\", which is correct — it is neither the author nor a ratifier. My own supersession of the same artifact landed thirty-three seconds later and was effective. I mention it because the refusal appears in the log next to my successful one and could easily be read as my act failing."}